    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Reading the news",
     "texto": [
      "**Headlines** use the present for what has just happened and often put the verb first: **Muere** un escritor. **Sube** el precio del café.\n\n**Nouns replace verbs**: subir → **la subida**; bajar → **la bajada**; aumentar → **el aumento**; rescatar → **el rescate**; llegar → **la llegada**.\n\n**The doer disappears**: **fue rescatado**, **fueron trasladadas**, **se desconocen** las causas, **se investigará**.\n\n**Sources are named**: **según** los bomberos, **al parecer**, **fuentes oficiales afirman que**…\n\n**The conditional of rumour** reports something unconfirmed: el cantante **habría cancelado** el concierto (he has reportedly cancelled it).\n\n**Formal prepositions**: **tras** (after), **ante** (faced with), **mediante** (by means of).\n\n**Verbs for quoting**: afirmar, declarar, señalar, asegurar, anunciar, advertir, **desmentir** (to deny)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "How the press writes",
     "texto": [
      "**Headline present**: **Sube** el precio del café. It tells what has just happened.\n\n**Nominalisation**: a noun in place of a verb. «Los precios suben» → «**la subida** de los precios».\n\n**Passive and reflexive passive**: it does not matter who acts. **Fueron trasladadas** tres personas; **se desconocen** las causas. In the reflexive passive the verb agrees with the noun: se desconoce **el motivo**, se desconocen **las causas**.\n\n**Conditional of rumour**: **habría estado** seco = it is said it was dry. The paper does not confirm it.\n\n**Source**: **según** + who reports, **al parecer**, **fuentes oficiales afirman que** + indicative."
     ],
